{"id":"oscr-earnings-q3-2026","title":"OSCR earnings — Oscar Health, Q3 2026","section":"Earnings","date":"2026-11-05","html":"<p>Management sees a flat marketplace in 2027 in which Oscar takes share and widens margins as rivals leave, and the thing that could spoil it is that the marketplace itself keeps shrinking and the people who stay are sicker.</p>\n<p>Reported Nov 5, 2026. Next report: not announced (one calendar site projects Feb 9, 2027). Updated Nov 12, 2026. Research, not investment advice.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-short-version\">The short version</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Demand:</strong> weaker. Membership was 2.69 million, up about 27% on the year but down in the quarter after a federal eligibility review; about 2.5 million expected at year-end.</li><li><strong>Guidance:</strong> raised, the third raise of operating earnings this year. Earnings from operations $850–950M (was $600–800M); revenue $19.0–19.3B (was $18.7–19.0B); medical loss ratio 80.5–81.5% (was 81.0–82.0%).</li><li><strong>Biggest worry:</strong> a shrinking, sicker ACA marketplace now that the enhanced subsidies are gone.</li><li><strong>Chart:</strong> no lean. Price sits in the middle of a $28.30 to $35.01 range; those two levels decide it. These are my lines, not yours.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"the-numbers\">The numbers</h3>\n<ul><li>Revenue $5.14B against $4.72B expected. EPS $0.49 against an expected loss of $0.11 to $0.17. A beat.</li><li>Read the beat with care: revenue included a $250M benefit from revised risk-adjustment estimates, which is larger than the whole quarter's $178M of operating earnings. Management said not all of it reaches profit because of offsetting claims.</li><li>Medical loss ratio 84.0%, down from 88.5% a year ago.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-management-said-about-the-road-ahead\">What management said about the road ahead</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>Rest of 2026:</strong> the raised range implies an operating loss in Q4, which is the normal seasonal pattern.</li><li><strong>2027 pricing:</strong> rate filings average about a 14% increase, driven mainly by medical cost trend (single transcript source).</li><li><strong>2027 market:</strong> overall marketplace roughly flat, with Oscar gaining share by entering more counties and picking up members from insurers that are leaving. A call write-up puts that pool at about 850,000 people.</li><li><strong>Margins:</strong> management says this will &quot;position us to grow market share and expand margin in 2027.&quot; Mark Bertolini, CEO.</li><li><strong>Membership:</strong> a second, smaller eligibility review is expected in Q4.</li><li><strong>Subsidies:</strong> the CEO does not expect the enhanced subsidies back in 2027.</li><li><strong>Formal 2027 guidance:</strong> comes on the Q4 call.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"what-management-is-worried-about\">What management is worried about</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>A smaller marketplace.</strong> Enhanced subsidies expired at the end of 2025 and cut participation; Oscar's own membership fell in the quarter and heads to about 2.5 million.</li><li><strong>Medical cost trend.</strong> The reason for the roughly 14% rate increase.</li><li><strong>Risk-adjustment estimates.</strong> A $250M swing in one quarter shows how large the uncertainty is. Nine-month operating cash flow of $3.5B included a $1.9B rise in amounts owed to the government programme.</li><li><strong>Identity verification on enrollment</strong> from Oct 12, 2026, which may reduce sign-ups.</li><li><strong>Drug tariffs.</strong> 100% on patented drugs could raise medical costs.</li></ol>\n<h3 id=\"the-big-worry-a-shrinking-sicker-marketplace-both-ways\">The big worry: a shrinking, sicker marketplace, both ways</h3>\n<p><strong>What would ease it</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Congress restoring the subsidies. The House passed a three-year extension 230–196 in January; the Senate never voted. After the midterms, Democrats reportedly plan an early vote in the new Congress, and the President has said he would veto their bills (a newsletter briefing; lower authority).</li><li>Rivals leaving. As of Oct 29, 11 insurers plan to exit at least one state for 2027 and Cigna is leaving the individual market entirely (KFF). Their members need a new plan.</li><li>Prices catching up with costs. Molina's CEO said the gap between rates and cost trend appears to have stabilised, and Elevance said the second-half cost rise is arriving more slowly than it feared.</li><li>Drug tariffs biting less than feared: the 17 largest drugmakers all signed deals that set their rate at 0% through January 2029, and generics are exempt.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>What would make it worse</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Enrollment falling further. The Congressional Budget Office sees marketplace enrollment at 13.1 million in 2027 against a 22.4 million peak in 2025 (via KFF). Oscar is planning for &quot;roughly flat&quot;.</li><li>Premium shock. The benchmark premium rises 13% for 2027 after 26% in 2026, about 42% in two years, and KFF says the sicker pool alone added about 4 points. Healthy people are the ones who drop out.</li><li>Tighter sign-up rules. First-time applicants and people paying $30 or less a month must now prove identity first; new broker registrations are frozen until February 2027; and a proposed rule would hold subsidies until eligibility is verified, from 2028 plans.</li><li>Medical trend running hotter. Molina raised its full-year cost trend outlook to 5.5% from 5% and expects its marketplace business to lose money in 2026.</li><li>A risk-adjustment bill going the other way. What was a $250M gain this quarter is an estimate until the government settles it next summer.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>How this company is placed against it</strong></p>\n<ul><li>It is a pure marketplace insurer. Centene, Molina and Elevance have other businesses to lean on; Oscar does not. Centene's marketplace membership fell to 3.4 million from 5.8 million in a year.</li><li>It is expanding (713 counties in 21 states) while Molina cuts its footprint from 14 states to 7. That is the share-gain case, and it is also the risk of inheriting costly members.</li><li>Cash is there but locked: about $9B of cash and investments, almost all inside the insurance subsidiaries.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"money-plans\">Money plans</h3>\n<ul><li>No buyback, no dividend.</li><li>Redeeming its remaining 2031 notes on Dec 31, 2026; $410M of 2030 convertible notes stay.</li><li>Capital spending $31.9M in nine months.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"people-and-governance\">People and governance</h3>\n<ul><li><strong>CEO:</strong> Mark Bertolini, 69, since April 2023. Not a founder. His contract was extended in December 2025 through April 1, 2029, with a $45M equity grant for 2026. He bought 1,000,000 shares from the company at $11.92 in April 2026.</li><li><strong>Founders:</strong> Mario Schlosser stepped back on June 1, 2026 from President of Technology to adviser to the CEO, and stays on the board. Joshua Kushner is Vice Chair.</li><li><strong>CFO:</strong> Scott Blackley, since August 2023.</li><li><strong>Successor named:</strong> not found. The board reviews succession plans at least quarterly.</li><li><strong>Control:</strong> dual-class. Class B shares carry 20 votes each. Thrive Capital and Joshua Kushner hold 68.0% of the votes; Schlosser 13.4% (2026 proxy). The proxy refers to a 2028 sunset of the dual-class structure; terms not found.</li><li><strong>Chair:</strong> independent and split from the CEO. Siddhartha Sankaran took over from Jeffery Boyd on June 4, 2026.</li><li><strong>Insider sales, as reported by InsiderTrades:</strong> Schlosser sold in several lots from November to January (the largest about $4.4M), and in early September the CFO sold about $908,000 and Schlosser about $988,000, described as tied to tax obligations. Investigations or lawsuits involving leadership in the last 12 months: not found.</li><li><strong>How much rests on one person:</strong> a lot. The turnaround is identified with a 69-year-old CEO on a contract to 2029, no successor is named, and one shareholder group controls the vote.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"does-the-chart-agree\">Does the chart agree?</h3>\n<figure class=\"chart\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" data-ticker=\"OSCR\" viewBox=\"0 0 760 400\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"OSCR · daily · my levels chart\" font-family=\"Arial, sans-serif\"><text x=\"8\" y=\"18\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"#20242C\" class=\"c-ink\">OSCR · daily · my levels</text><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"374.0\" y2=\"374.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"289.0\" y2=\"289.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"204.0\" y2=\"204.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"119.0\" y2=\"119.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"8\" x2=\"610\" y1=\"34.0\" y2=\"34.0\" stroke=\"#E3DACB\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-grid\"/><line x1=\"10.3\" x2=\"10.3\" y1=\"288.1\" y2=\"356.5\" stroke=\"#2F6E4A\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-up\"/><rect x=\"8.9\" y=\"292.5\" width=\"2.9\" height=\"58.5\" fill=\"#2F6E4A\" class=\"c-up\"/><line x1=\"14.9\" x2=\"14.9\" y1=\"275.4\" y2=\"321.5\" stroke=\"#A63A2E\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-dn\"/><rect x=\"13.5\" y=\"292.3\" width=\"2.9\" height=\"4.0\" fill=\"#A63A2E\" class=\"c-dn\"/><line x1=\"19.6\" x2=\"19.6\" y1=\"250.2\" y2=\"293.8\" stroke=\"#A63A2E\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-dn\"/><rect x=\"18.1\" y=\"261.3\" width=\"2.9\" height=\"21.0\" fill=\"#A63A2E\" class=\"c-dn\"/><line x1=\"24.2\" x2=\"24.2\" y1=\"247.5\" y2=\"295.6\" stroke=\"#2F6E4A\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-up\"/><rect x=\"22.8\" y=\"258.8\" width=\"2.9\" height=\"31.7\" fill=\"#2F6E4A\" class=\"c-up\"/><line x1=\"28.8\" x2=\"28.8\" y1=\"259.9\" y2=\"289.5\" stroke=\"#A63A2E\" stroke-width=\"1\" class=\"c-dn\"/><rect x=\"27.4\" y=\"265.0\" width=\"2.9\" height=